(a) Every effort is made in the local employment and selective service offices to supply domestic help or a practical nurse to those homes where a mother is ill and children are dependent upon her care. It has been arranged to have orders for domestic help in such instances endorsed by a doctor, social agency, or health service, and the urgent neces-

sity of filling orders which have been endorsed in this way has been impressed upon the local offices.
(b) Hospitals have been given highest labour priority, and in every instance, are on an equal basis with war industry. Hospitals have been given the privilege of advertising for, interviewing, and employing their staff personnel without reference to the local selective service and employment office except to send a report of placements by mail to the offices. A directive sent to the local employment and selective service offices emphasizes the following points:
1.	A conference should be held with the hospitals and institutions in your district to determine the maximum labour requirements to provide a full complement of non-professional employees. Such an estimate should include both men and women employees and care should be exercised to make use of women in any capacity where their services would be adequate.
2.	At the time of the conference the hospitals should be informed that it might take a little time to meet the full requirements. The attention of the hospital representatives should be drawn to any major difficulties the office has experienced in securing employees, such' as transportation, differentiation of wage rates within the same district, hours of work which prevent the referral of numbers of women applying at the office, etc., in order that the hospitals will fully understand the situation and possibly make the adjustment which would place them on an equal basis with other hospitals or other service occupations.
3.	If, in the opinion of the local office, it is impossible to meet maximum demands, the local office manager should consult with the regional office, relative to the advisability of making special plans for intensive recruiting. Any proposed plans may be used only with the approval and under the direction of the regional office. The regional superintendent will arrange for discussions with the provincial department of health, hospital associations, provincial secretaries of the various women's institutes or other organizations with a view to seeking their cooperation in the recruiting campaign for additional hospital workers.
4.	Following are suggestions which may be followed by local offices in arranging a campaign for the workers required. It is not intended that local offices should confine themselves to the suggestions listed below but rather it is expected that local offices will develop additional plans particularly suited to the local area:

Questions as Orders for Returns
In any centre where there is a list of unemployed women, whether housewives or not, a group should be selected by the local office and a representative of the hospital should be asked to address them on the nature of the work required;
Any persons registered in the women's registration of September, 1942, particularly those in the rural areas who might be considered suitable, should be informed of definite positions in hospitals;
If required, at a particular time, definite publicity should be given out through the newspapers and an appeal made by radio;
Representatives of hospitals should be asked to secure publicity and necessary advertising in such a manner and at such times as will aid in the recruitment of the required employees;
A statement on the hours of work, wages and perquisites should be drawn up and provided to any group cooperating in the campaign. This information should be distributed through the urban and rural areas as may be considered advisable;
Particular emphasis should be given to the fact that many of the shortages can be overcome by the use of part-time help. Revisions can be made in the hours of employment and the adoption of short shifts can help overcome labour shortages. The hospital should be requested to designate some person on their staff as the "contact" with the selective service office;	.
The local office should thoroughly comb the list of unemployed men, particularly those in the old age brackets some of whom might be suitable for this type of work, either on a part-time or full-time basis;
The cooperation of the urban and rural women's organizations and churches should be secured to carry out the recruiting programme;
Men's organizations interested in employment of their members should also be asked for assistance. This is particularly true of veterans' organizations who are anxious to place their men;
The veterans' welfare officer should also be interested in this campaign as many of the men coming to him for assistance may be suitable for hospital employment.
It is desirable that in each local and regional office one or more persons as required should be assigned to deal particularly with hospital requirements.

I would respectfully refer the hon. member to the reports of the board, which have been tabled in this house and are available, and which enumerate in detail the different applications made to the board, giving * a report of the hearings and the decisions. They are quite numerous. The report contains sixty-three pages, and I cannot be expected to read it all. It was filed and it must have come to the notice or the attention of the hon. member.
Let me give some examples of the matters which come under the attention of the board:
General order 635, an order made on January 12, 1943, with regard to the transportation by express of acids, inflammables, oxidizing substances, samples of explosives, et cetera;
Another ruling made in January, 1943, in the matter of the Express Traffic Association of Canada for permission to make shipments of trench mortar bombs, methods of boxing, packaging, weight, and methods of loading on cars;
Another order made on October 30, 1942, respecting the matter of transportation of explosives;
An application made by the Railway Association of Canada for an order amending the uniform rules governing the determination of visual acuity, colour perception, and hearing of railway employees;
Order amending regulations for the transportation of explosives and other dangerous articles by freight;
Applications made by the Great Northern and Northern Pacific railway companies and

Supply-T ransport
their subsidiary companies for an order approving the conditions limiting liability to be printed on their free annual and trip transportation over their lines;
Regarding emergency shipments by railway express of ammunition;
Application by the Express Traffic Association of Canada re handling of samples of explosive ;
Again, rules regarding the transportation of explosives, and the use of black powder kegs for transportation of black powder;
Application to permit the transportation of explosives in paper bags;
Again, transportation of explosives and other dangerous articles;
Regarding rates to be charged for transporting individuals over the railroads.
There are a large number of orders which were made by the board on application of several interests, parties, railway associations, manufacturers, and railway employees.

Hon, J. L. ILSLEY (Minister of Finance) moved that the house go into committee) to consider the following resolution:
That in order to promote the construction of new houses, the repair and modernization of existing houses, the improvement of housing and living conditions and the expansion of employment in the post-war period, it is expedient to introduce a measure to authorize the Minister of Finance:
1.	to join with approved lending institutions in the making of loans under certain conditions for the construction of homes for prospective home-owners, and the sharing of losses, if any, in respect of such loans, the aggregate amount of the advances .to be made not to exceed one hundred million dollars;
2.	to join with approved lending institutions in the making of loans under certain conditions for the construction of houses to be rented to tenants and the sharing of losses, if any, in respect of such loans, and to make loans to limited dividend housing corporations under certain conditions to assist in the financing of low rental housing projects, the aggregate amount of the advances to ibe made by the minister for the construction of houses for rental purposes not to exceed fifty million dollars; to authorize life insurance companies under the jurisdiction of parliament to purchase land and construct thereon and manage low cost or moderate cost rental housing projects subject to certain conditions and to authorize certain guarantees in respect of such investments, and to authorize the Minister of Finance under certain conditions to make grants to municipalities, up to an aggregate amount not exceeding twenty million dollars, for the purpose of assisting in the clearance of slums or blighted areas;
3.	to provide special assistance in the contracts with approved lending institutions to encourage the making of loans in rural areas, and to enter into contracts with manufacturers of building equipment to assure the production of components for rural homes on an economical cost basis, provided that the gross [Mr. Ian Mackenzie.!
liability of the dominion in respect of such contracts with manufacturers of components for rural homes outstanding at any one time shall not exceed five million dollars;
4.	to guarantee under certain conditions loans for the repair, alteration and extension of existing homes to an aggregate amount of one hundred million dollars;
5.	to carry on and promote technical research and investigations designed to lower housing costs and promote better housing conditions and the more efficient planting of communities;
and further to provide that the moneys required for the making of the said loans and grants and the payment of losses incurred under any guarantee or other contract authorized .by the act shall be paid out of any unappropriated moneys in the consolidated revenue fund and that all other moneys payable under the act, including the salaries of technical and clerical staffs and all other expenses of administration, shall be paid out of moneys appropriated by parliament for the purposes of the act.

Mr. Speaker, I regret the imperative necessity of' introducing anything which might interfere with the full enjoyment by the Prime Minister (Mr. Mackenzie King) of the tributes and encomiums of his friends, but the welfare of our returning wounded transcends in importance all other considerations.
I therefore ask for leave to move the adjournment of the house to consider a matter of urgent public importance, namely: the grave emergency which the premier of Ontario in a statement to the press and a letter to the Prime Minister declares has arisen because of the lack of proper hospital accommodation for our returning wounded;
That as a consequence of this critical situation, even men who have lost arms or legs and still need hospital treatment, are being given leave to their homes to provide beds for stretcher cases immediately expected;
That because of the lack of adequate accommodation and organization, many serious cases have Tailed to receive the prompt and adequate treatment which they need;
And that the situation is extremely serious.

The loans which can be made under this bill can be made only to industrial enterprises.

(d) "industrial enterprise" means a business in which the manufacture, processing or refrigeration of goods, wares and merchandise or the building of ships or vessels or the generating or distributing of electricity is carried on;
I did not hear the speech of my colleague the Minister of Munitions and Supply to which the hon. member refers, and I should not like to comment on it unless I see a report of it.

I do not imagine they were attested until they came back.
Australia has not as yet completed a war service gratuity plan. I have some information with regard to plans that were under consideration, but the information is confidential at the moment. As I indicated yesterday, South Africa has provided for a flat rate for all ranks regardless of the area of service;
whether they serve in South Africa or anywhere else it is the same-thirty shillings a month for each month of service for European men; fifteen shillings a month for European women; ten shillings a month for coloured personnel and five shillings a month for native personnel. The payment in these cases is to be made in union loan certificates. The house may be interested in this. In going over the reports of 1918 and 1919 I happened to find- the government claims no pride of authorship of this plan-but as a matter of fact it was after we had evolved the plan that we found that a similar plan was proposed by the late Colonel J. W. Margeson, of Nova Scotia by the way. He presented it to the civil reestablishment committee in 1918 or 1919. He proposed a plan whereby everybody would be paid up to 8500 in cash, and

It is addressed to the houses of parliament. I think it is perfectly in order.
That whereas the Prime Minister of Canada has pledged on behalf of his government to the House of Commons that if "circumstances should arise which would render the use of compulsion imperative, such for example as the maintenance of the necessary reinforcements for Canada's army overseas", the compulsory provisions of Bill 80 would become effective by order in council;
And whereas such circumstances have arisen;
